## Three claims in the triage brief that were WRONG, stated plainly

The brief asserted these from titles and campaign memory. Two are wrong and one
is imprecise; all three were checked against `origin/main` and committed data.

1. **"#471 is one of the four waiting-policy arms #504 measured as NULL."**
   WRONG — see the OPEN section above. Different cell, different thread count,
   and the thread count that matters is on #504's own not-measured list.

2. **"#469's column-sharding is the shape #482's column-compact `ReconBand`
   landed."** IMPRECISE in a way that matters. #469's mechanism is a TRACKER
   change — `RowMap`, per-record `c0`/`c1` column bands, `cols_meet`, `COL_ANY`,
   `StridedRows` — and **none of those symbols exist anywhere on `main`.**
   #482's `src/owned_recon.rs` is an OWNED BUFFER whose uniqueness is proved by
   borrowck with no record at all, one band per WORKER rather than per tile, and
   it removes recon's registrations instead of making them column-aware. The
   thing that actually generalises #469's "one record for a rectangle" is
   **#505/#513's exact strided-rectangle record**, and that covers the FILTER
   CHAIN, a different population from #482's. So #469's goal was reached by two
   later changes, neither of which is its mechanism. (#469 also shipped Miri UB
   — an exact record behind a hull reference — which is what #470/#472 found.)

3. **"#472 ... is what #505's exact strided-rectangle record actually
   implements and #513 shipped by default."** CORRECT, and verified:
   `DisjointMutRectGuard`/`DisjointImmutRectGuard` hand out one row at a time
   via `row_mut(&mut self)` (`lib.rs:1615`), and `Cargo.toml` now declares
   `__lf_rect` and `__rows_rect` as retained no-ops because "the rectangle is
   now the DEFAULT path (2026-08-11)". #472's OTHER half — the `macos-15-intel`
   `compact_read_per_row` overlap panic it analysed and could not fix without an
   x86 box — was independently found, root-caused and FIXED as the #494 t=8
   race, recorded at `X64_APPLICABILITY.md` §G. #472 was right that the defect
   was on `main` and not an artefact of its own change.

## Two stale conclusions on the closed branches, for anyone who reads them

* **#480 concluded "the +0.71 ns is paid by EVERY registration"** and left
  `add`'s two extra parameters as the only unexamined suspect. #506 refuted
  that: the t=1 cost is **CODE PLACEMENT**. 4,828 bytes of provably-dead
  `#[used]` text, 0 symbols resized, costs +1.10% at t=1 on `v4k8tile`, 0 of 11;
  against a SAME-SOURCE control the rectangle costs **0.9967 (7/9)**. #480's
  ruled-out list (the rectangle code path at 1.0005 with the path provably dead,
  the column stores, the row map) stands and is useful; its residual
  attribution does not. Judge any t=1 claim inside ~1.5% against a same-source
  arm or a `__pad_text` rung, never against `main`'s binary.

* **#485 and #488 both proposed reducing a registration COUNT**, and #505 has
  since corrected the cost model they were priced against: cost tracks
  **distinct shard lines a worker visits**, not records filed. `LfBlock::fill`
  files 8.98 records per 2.09 shard lines and is 31.7% of the population for
  3.9-4.4% of tracker CPU, so the DENSEST site is the least valuable count cut
  available. Both PRs reached "not worth it" by measurement anyway, which is why
  neither is contradicted — but a future reader should not take their framing
  ("this site is 33.6% of registrations") as a priority ordering.

## Scope

Records only. No decoder code, no `crates/` behaviour, no feature flags landed
by this round beyond the #448 test vector in #517. Every PR's code stays on its
branch at the head SHA in the table above. Nothing was re-measured: another
agent holds the box for timed work in a sibling repo, so every figure here is
cited from a committed record, from a CI log, or counted from the tree.
